WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:14.460
MICROSOFT EDGE:
Ah, internet explorer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:27.840
the browser you use to download other browsers.&nbsp;
Or at least, that’s what it used to be until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.120
Microsoft finally decided to retire Internet&nbsp;
Explorer in mid 2022. Don’t worry though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.220
Microsoft has a replacement for Internet&nbsp;
Explorer called Edge, and it’s able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.800
download Google Chrome even faster. In fact,&nbsp;
it’s able to download it even faster the Chrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.840
would be able to download it given that it’s&nbsp;
objectively faster. According to Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:55.920
Edge is 112% faster than Chrome, but who knows&nbsp;
what cherry picked data they’re using. Something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:02.520
that is surprising though is the Edge is actually&nbsp;
faster in real world testing as well. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.320
what many blogs have beeng claiming for quite&nbsp;
some time now, and through my personal testing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:13.260
it does appear to be true. On BrowserBench,&nbsp;
I was able to get a score of 270 using Edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:20.220
while I only got a score 251 using Chrome. This&nbsp;
wasn’t just a fluke either. Test after test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:26.040
Edge consistently scored about 20 points better&nbsp;
than chrome. Edge is not just snappier than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:32.820
chrome either, it’s also more efficient. With&nbsp;
60 tabs open, Chrome used up 3.7 GB of memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:39.060
while Edge only used up 2.9 GB. This will be&nbsp;
quite noticeable for all you who regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:45.540
have dozens of tabs open. I know you’re watching.&nbsp;
Alright, so Edge might be marginally faster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.400
more efficient than the other browsers, but&nbsp;
speed isn’t the only factor when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.100
choosing a browser. There’s several other factors&nbsp;
that you might consider such as which one is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.080
socially acceptable. I’m just kidding, of course&nbsp;
you don’t do that, but what you might consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:07.500
is the default search engine. Now technically,&nbsp;
the default search engine on Edge is Bing. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.600
Microsoft isn’t hell bent on getting you to&nbsp;
actually use Bing. In fact, you can switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:18.360
to Google within literally 10 seconds. But, what&nbsp;
about the hundreds of thousands of extensions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:24.120
are available on the chrome web store. Surely,&nbsp;
Microsoft can’t compete with that right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.460
they don’t have to because they simply let you&nbsp;
use the chrome web store. The new version of Edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:35.460
is based on Chromium meaning that you can add&nbsp;
any Chrome extension to Edge. You can also add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.660
Microsoft extensions to Edge as well, not that&nbsp;
Microsoft’s catalog is anything to brag about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:45.240
but you do have that as well which simply gives&nbsp;
you even more options. On top of all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:51.780
Edge also has awesome integration with Microsoft&nbsp;
Office and and Outlook for obvious reasons. Edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.460
also comes with a lot of quirks that many people&nbsp;
really like such as the ability to arrange your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.340
tabs vertically and the sidebar on the side of&nbsp;
the screen. So, really, you get the best of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:08.700
the Google world and the Microsoft world. This&nbsp;
is really not that surprising given that Edge is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.260
really just a faster implementation of Chrome with&nbsp;
some Microsoft aspects thrown in here and there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:18.840
But, despite this, Edge is getting destroyed&nbsp;
when it comes to market share. Chrome’s market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:25.800
share stands at a dominating 60% while Edge’s&nbsp;
market share isn’t even able to cross 5%. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.600
why does no one want Edge despite it&nbsp;
being equivalent to chrome if not better.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.320
INTERNET EXPLORER:&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.440
One of the main reasons that people avoid Edge&nbsp;
like the plague is because of Internet Explorer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:46.380
At one point in history, Internet Explorer was&nbsp;
actaully the most popular browser on the planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:53.400
but not because it was good but because it was&nbsp;
forced. You see, before Internet Explorer, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:59.940
were perfectly happy using the WorldWideWeb,&nbsp;
Lynx, and of course Netscape. But, in 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.700
Microsoft decided that they wanted a piece of&nbsp;
the internet pie as well, so they came out with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.320
Internet Explorer. And knowing Microsoft, they&nbsp;
didn’t want to leave the adoption of Internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:16.440
Explorer up to chance. So, they bundled Internet&nbsp;
Explorer in with Windows. And just as they hoped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:22.260
new PC users tended to use the default browser,&nbsp;
Internet Explorer, and this led to the browser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:28.560
becoming the dominant browser. But Microsoft had&nbsp;
gotten one thing wrong. Just because Internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:35.040
Explorer was the most popular didn’t mean that&nbsp;
it was actually popular amongst people. At best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.860
people had a neutral opinion on the browser&nbsp;
while enthusiasts hated it. This wasn’t to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:46.380
say that Internet Explorer was terrible. It&nbsp;
got the job done fine, but at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.900
it wasn’t anything notable either. What really&nbsp;
triggered enthusiasts was the fact that Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:57.480
forced Internet Explorer while there were several&nbsp;
other great options. It wasn’t just enthusiasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.160
that were triggered either. Regulators were&nbsp;
triggered as well. Microsoft was literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.080
blocking computer manufacturers from putting&nbsp;
any other web browser on the desktop. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.720
was after they agreed that they wouldn’t bundle&nbsp;
Internet Explorer in with Windows. As you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.120
guess, regulators didn’t take this violation&nbsp;
lightly, and this issue would be escalated up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.560
to the supreme court. During the worst of it,&nbsp;
Microsoft was actually being ordered to split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.260
into two different companies. But, luckily for&nbsp;
Microsoft, this order was cancelled at the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.660
second because the judge issuing the order had&nbsp;
broken protocol by talking to the press. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:39.540
ironically, it was the press that saved Microsoft&nbsp;
at the Supreme Court, but they by no means helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:45.660
Microsoft in the public court. By the early 2000s,&nbsp;
the general public was well aware of how Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:52.020
had abused their power with IE and they resented&nbsp;
Microsoft for it. For many, Microsoft was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:57.120
embodiment of everything that was wrong with a&nbsp;
capitalistic society. So, they started leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:02.820
in droves, and they started supporting an open&nbsp;
source project: Mozilla Firefox. For many years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:09.120
FireFox was the go to browser not just for&nbsp;
enthusiasts, but for everyone. By 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:15.960
FireFox had 33% market while IE had 50% market&nbsp;
share. In fact, if Firefox had a few more years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:22.260
they would’ve overtaken IE and became the most&nbsp;
popular browser in the world by far. But, Sundar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:27.720
Pichai and Google had a different plan. They had&nbsp;
seen quite a bit of success within the search and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:33.180
email space, and now they wanted to challenge the&nbsp;
likes of Microsoft and Apple with Google Chrome.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:39.660
GOOGLE CHROME:
While Firefox was giving Microsoft a run for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:45.600
money, they had by no means lost the game quite&nbsp;
yet. All of the F Microsofters had already left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.160
so the people that were left were simply looking&nbsp;
for a functional browser, and Internet Explorer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.200
was more than capable of that. So, all Microsoft&nbsp;
had to do was keep Internet Explorer functional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:02.005
but they couldn’t even do that. At first glance,&nbsp;
this might be a bit surprising. Why was a multi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.005 --> 00:07:07.680
hundred billion dollar company not able to keep up&nbsp;
with a browser? A literal opensource community was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:15.900
able to do that. Well, the answer is not a lack of&nbsp;
resources, the answer is hubris and arrogance. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:22.140
see, Microsoft had never played by the rules, they&nbsp;
were the ones that set the rules. And everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:27.840
else had to follow Microsoft’s rules whether they&nbsp;
wanted to or not because Microsoft had the users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.360
Microsoft approached IE with the same attitude.&nbsp;
They didn’t give a crap about global web standards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:40.020
or developers or even putting out consistent&nbsp;
updates. Microsoft was updating IE only once every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:46.320
few years. While developers were willing to put up&nbsp;
with this BS when IE had 80 to 90% market share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.880
they stopped catering to Microsoft’s needs&nbsp;
as Firefox and Chrome starting making a name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:58.620
for themselves. Unlike IE, Chrome was built for&nbsp;
developers by developers. Google figured that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:04.080
get end users to switch, they first needed to get&nbsp;
developers to switch and make websites that ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:09.120
great on their browser. Fortunately for Google,&nbsp;
this wasn’t that hard to do given that Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.940
was in hibernation. The results of this weren’t&nbsp;
immediately clear, but about 5 years after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.160
launch of Chrome in the early to mid 2010s, the&nbsp;
general public started to notice. More and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:25.500
websites started to look wonky on IE. People&nbsp;
also started to notice that their friends who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.660
used Chrome experienced less lag, fewer crashes,&nbsp;
and simply had an overall smoother experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:36.240
But IE’s problems stretched much further&nbsp;
than just being slow and displaying websites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:42.300
poorly. Do you remember how computer viruses were&nbsp;
an actual concern 10 to 20 years ago? Well, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:48.060
of the main reasons for that is actually Internet&nbsp;
Explorer. You see, Microsoft allowed developers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:54.180
write website code in the international standard&nbsp;
which is Javascript or Visual Basic Script. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:58.980
reasoning for this was that VB Script could allow&nbsp;
developers to accomplish more advanced tasks as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:04.800
they would have greater access to the user’s PC.&nbsp;
But, this also meant that it was way easier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:11.220
infect computers with viruses. Let’s just say that&nbsp;
this security issue was so bad that Microsoft has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:17.940
since put out tutorials on how to disable VBScript&nbsp;
execution. So, not only was IE slower and wonky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:23.100
it was a serious security threat. I mean,&nbsp;
it’s almost as if Microsoft was actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:29.700
encouraging people to leave IE. And that’s&nbsp;
exactly what they did. Between 2009 and 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:37.560
IE’s marketshare would plummet from 65% down to&nbsp;
15%. Clearly, Microsoft’s hibernation strategy was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.840
no longer working, and they would finally decide&nbsp;
to do something with the launch of Microsoft Edge.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.560
INTERNET EXPLORER V2:&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:53.100
Microsoft initially launched Edge alongside&nbsp;
Windows 10 back in 2015, but while Edge had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.780
much more modern look than IE, not much had&nbsp;
changed really changed. Behind the scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:03.000
Microsoft was making all of the same mistakes&nbsp;
that they were making with IE. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.820
Edge was built on Microsoft’s proprietary&nbsp;
browser engine, EdgeHTML and their Chakra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:15.060
Javascript Engine. While these sounds fancy, the&nbsp;
reality was that Edge still didn’t support even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:22.440
basic open media standards such as WebM and Opus.&nbsp;
So, Edge was really just the same crappy Internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:28.140
Explorer but with a face lift. We should also&nbsp;
mention that Microsoft was still using their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:34.020
classic monopolistic practices with Edge as it&nbsp;
came with Windows 10 and now Windows 11 as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:40.740
But, by this point, people had become numb to&nbsp;
Microsoft’s games. Downloading a new browser using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:46.440
Microsoft’s default browser became a natural part&nbsp;
of buying a new computer. Nobody even bothered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:53.640
checking out if Edge was actually good or not. In&nbsp;
fact, Edge’s market share was a laughable 2%. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:59.340
no worries, it’s not like any of these people&nbsp;
missed out on anything because Edge still sucked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:04.440
This didn’t remain the case forever though as&nbsp;
Satya Nadella, the new CEO, would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:09.420
come around to addressing Edge. Unlike his&nbsp;
predecessors, Satya didn’t have the pompous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:15.360
viewpoint that Microsoft was the only and best&nbsp;
choice consumers had. Instead, he acknowledged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.700
their shortcomings and embraced their competitors.&nbsp;
For example, instead of endlessly pursuing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:25.740
Windows Phone, he chose to embrace iOS and Android&nbsp;
by launching Microsoft Office on these platforms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:30.900
He would carry over this same mindset when it&nbsp;
come to addressing the browser space. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:34.440
of trying to outshine Chrome by developing&nbsp;
their own advanced browser from scratch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:40.440
he chose to embrace Chrome. He acknowledged that&nbsp;
Chrome had won over the users and the developers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:46.440
so it was much smarter to build off of Chrome’s&nbsp;
success and simply add a Windows spin to it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:51.180
that’s exactly what happened. At the end of&nbsp;
2018, Microsoft announced that they would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:55.800
switching Edge to Chromium. And at beginning&nbsp;
of 2020, Microsoft would launch the new Edge.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:56.760
THE NEW EDGE:&nbsp;

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:06.300
Since then, Edge has simply become more and&nbsp;
more integrated with Chrome but at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:12.060
time it has also become more and more unique.&nbsp;
For example, they embraced the chrome webstore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:17.760
but at the same time, they also addressed&nbsp;
Chrome’s atrocious ram usage. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:23.460
they let you use Google as your default search&nbsp;
engine, but at the same time, you have the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:28.560
power of Microsoft’s Office suite which let’s&nbsp;
be honest is way better than Google’s offerings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:34.380
Microsoft Edge is no longer the lock into the&nbsp;
Microsoft ecosystem that it once used to be. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:41.220
despite all this, no one wants to switch primarily&nbsp;
because of their negative opinion on IE and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:45.960
original Edge. With that being said though, we&nbsp;
can’t expect these types of switches to happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:51.780
overnight. Enthusiasts are starting notice, and&nbsp;
as Google becomes the one that’s hibernating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:56.940
we may just see a non monopolistic browser&nbsp;
space. Would you be wiling to switch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:02.340
Edge? Comment that down below. Also, drop a&nbsp;
like if you’re glad that Chrome may finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:07.260
see some competition. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:11.520
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
